\section*{Appendix B: Gradient Solver Deep Dive}

\subsection*{B.1 Overview}

The gradient solver computes the atomic stability gradient
\begin{equation}
    \nabla \Phi_{A}(t,\mathbf{x}) =
    \Phi_{A,ext}(t,\mathbf{x}) - \Phi_{A,int}(t,\mathbf{x}),
\end{equation}
and refines it using magnetohydrodynamic (MHD) stability, Coulomb-barrier
modulation, and atomic flux--velocity coupling. The solver produces the
reinforced gradient $\nabla \Phi_{A}^{*}$ used in the FAE motion law.

\subsection*{B.2 Field Decomposition}

We decompose each field into scalar and vector components:
\begin{equation}
    \Phi_{A,ext} = \phi_{ext} + \mathbf{A}_{ext} \cdot \mathbf{x},
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
    \Phi_{A,int} = \phi_{int} + \mathbf{A}_{int} \cdot \mathbf{x},
\end{equation}
where $\phi$ represents scalar atomic stability and $\mathbf{A}$ represents
directional atomic bias.

Thus,
\begin{equation}
    \nabla \Phi_{A} =
    \nabla(\phi_{ext} - \phi_{int})
    + (\mathbf{A}_{ext} - \mathbf{A}_{int}).
\end{equation}

\subsection*{B.3 MHD Stability Contribution}

The MHD correction term is:
\begin{equation}
    \Delta_{\text{MHD}} =
    \nabla \cdot \left( \frac{\mathbf{B}\mathbf{B}}{\mu_{0}} \right)
    - \nabla\left(\frac{B^{2}}{2\mu_{0}}\right),
\end{equation}
which stabilizes $\Phi_{A,int}$ by suppressing turbulent plasma behavior.

The corrected internal field is:
\begin{equation}
    \Phi_{A,int}^{\prime} =
    \Phi_{A,int} + \beta_{\text{MHD}} \Delta_{\text{MHD}}.
\end{equation}

\subsection*{B.4 Coulomb-Barrier Modulation}

For $Z = 76$--$92$, the Coulomb barrier modifies the gradient via:
\begin{equation}
    \Delta_{Z} =
    \frac{Z e^{2}}{4\pi\epsilon_{0} r^{2}} \hat{r},
\end{equation}
where $r$ is the effective nuclear separation.

The external field becomes:
\begin{equation}
    \Phi_{A,ext}^{\prime} =
    \Phi_{A,ext} + \gamma_{Z} \Delta_{Z}.
\end{equation}

\subsection*{B.5 Atomic Flux--Velocity Coupling}

Atomic flux $\mathbf{u}$ contributes:
\begin{equation}
    \Delta_{u} =
    \gamma_{A} (\nabla \Phi_{A} \cdot \mathbf{u}) \hat{u},
\end{equation}
which biases the gradient toward regions of high atomic flow.

Thus the raw gradient becomes:
\begin{equation}
    \nabla \Phi_{A}^{\prime} =
    \nabla \Phi_{A}
    + \beta_{\text{MHD}} \Delta_{\text{MHD}}
    + \gamma_{Z} \Delta_{Z}
    + \gamma_{A} \Delta_{u}.
\end{equation}

\subsection*{B.6 Entropy Reinforcement}

Entropy reinforcement deepens the gradient:
\begin{equation}
    \nabla \Phi_{A}^{*} =
    \nabla \Phi_{A}^{\prime}
    \left(1 + \alpha_{S} S_{A}(t)\right),
\end{equation}
where $S_{A}(t)$ is derived from fusion energy spikes.

\subsection*{B.7 Stability Conditions}

The solver enforces:
\begin{equation}
    \left|\nabla \Phi_{A}^{*}\right| < \Lambda_{\max},
\end{equation}
to prevent runaway gradients.

Additionally, the solver requires:
\begin{equation}
    \frac{\partial}{\partial t}
    \left|\nabla \Phi_{A}^{*}\right| \ge 0,
\end{equation}
for monotonic gradient strengthening.

\subsection*{B.8 Final Gradient}

The final gradient used in the motion law is:
\begin{equation}
    \boxed{
    \nabla \Phi_{A}^{*} =
    \left[
        \nabla(\phi_{ext} - \phi_{int})
        + (\mathbf{A}_{ext} - \mathbf{A}_{int})
        + \beta_{\text{MHD}} \Delta_{\text{MHD}}
        + \gamma_{Z} \Delta_{Z}
        + \gamma_{A} \Delta_{u}
    \right]
    (1 + \alpha_{S} S_{A})
    }
\end{equation}

This expression defines the full atomic-gradient structure driving FAE drift.